# Ubuntu Determinant Git message & concern configuration (example).
#
# A single, reviewable document for the human-facing text the Edition Git emits
# on stdout and stderr, and for the *concerns* those messages describe (memory
# bloat, disk-space exhaustion, missing files, and the like).
#
# Format: UTF-8, line-oriented `KEY: value`. Blank lines and `#` comments are
# ignored. Keys are case-insensitive; for a given message or concern the first
# occurrence of a scalar key wins. Sections are opened by a `[NAME]` header and
# continue until the next header. This mirrors the conventions of
# GIT_OPERATIONS.logic and git/propath.config.example.
#
# The recommended location is `.gitmessages` at the repository root (see
# MESSAGES.md). This file mirrors the native enums and default catalog in
# git/messages.h; the command surface (git-workflow.sh) reads it as a reference
# and input.
#
# Safety. This document only re-words, re-streams, or localizes output. A stale,
# missing, or altered config falls back to the compiled defaults in messages.h;
# it can never turn an error into a success, suppress a safety refusal, route a
# diagnostic away from stderr to hide it, change a failing exit status into a
# passing one, or authorize a destructive action.

MESSAGES-VERSION: 1

# =============================================================================
# CONCERNS
# =============================================================================
# A concern is the underlying condition an operation may need to report; a
# message (below) is how that condition is phrased. Each concern has a settled
# definition and a default severity. Concern keys:
#
#   DEFINE   - the mature, one-line definition of the concern.
#   SEVERITY - default severity when this concern is reported:
#              info | note | warning | error | fatal.
#   STREAM   - default stream: stdout | stderr. Diagnostics (note and above)
#              default to stderr; results default to stdout. A concern of
#              error/fatal severity may not be routed to stdout.
#   DETECT   - (optional) the observable signal(s) that indicate the concern;
#              advisory documentation only, never a promise of detection.

# --- Resource concerns -------------------------------------------------------
[CONCERN memory-bloat]
DEFINE: Resident/working-set memory growth beyond the advisory budget for an operation.
SEVERITY: warning
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: Working-set estimate exceeds the per-operation advisory budget.

[CONCERN disk-space]
DEFINE: Insufficient or nearly-exhausted local disk space to complete an operation safely.
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A write fails with ENOSPC, or free space falls below the required estimate.

[CONCERN size-ceiling]
DEFINE: A planned unit exceeds a policy size ceiling (such as the 200 MiB push/transaction budget) and must not be split.
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: An ordered item or transaction measures larger than the compiled ceiling.

[CONCERN arithmetic-overflow]
DEFINE: A size or count computation would overflow; it is rejected rather than allowed to wrap.
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A running total or rewrite/eviction count would exceed its integer range.

# --- Integrity concerns ------------------------------------------------------
[CONCERN missing-file]
DEFINE: An expected file, path, or object is absent.
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A required path or object id cannot be resolved.

[CONCERN corruption]
DEFINE: A file or object failed an integrity or fsck check and may be damaged.
SEVERITY: fatal
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A checksum, fsck, or object-parse check fails.

[CONCERN provenance-gap]
DEFINE: Required provenance (author, committer, parent, or reference) is absent; it is recorded as absent, never invented.
SEVERITY: note
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: An expected provenance field is missing when a record is emitted.

# --- Precondition concerns ---------------------------------------------------
[CONCERN permission]
DEFINE: A filesystem or transport permission prevents the requested action.
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: An open, write, or transport step fails with a permission error.

[CONCERN precondition]
DEFINE: A required state is unmet (not a repository, detached HEAD, a dirty tree, nothing staged, a missing tool, or a malformed argument).
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A guard check fails before the operation begins.

[CONCERN network-loss]
DEFINE: A slow, lossy, or interrupted connection. This is a normal condition handled by resume, not a failure in itself.
SEVERITY: note
STREAM: stderr
DETECT: A transport attempt is interrupted or the remote acknowledges fewer units than sent.

# =============================================================================
# MESSAGES
# =============================================================================
# Each message is addressed by a stable ID (matching enum git_msg_id in
# messages.h). Message keys:
#
#   TEXT     - the wording. Required; must be non-empty. Careful and mature:
#              state the condition, say what the tool did or declined to do,
#              and where useful how to proceed. No blame and no exclamation.
#   STREAM   - stdout | stderr. Defaults from the message's concern.
#   SEVERITY - info | note | warning | error | fatal. Defaults from the concern.
#   CONCERN  - the concern this message reports (one of the names above, or
#              `none` for ordinary success/progress).
#
# A message of error/fatal severity may not be routed to stdout; such a config
# is rejected and the compiled default is used instead.

# --- Environment / preconditions (shared) ------------------------------------
[MESSAGE git-required]
CONCERN: precondition
SEVERITY: error
STREAM: stderr
TEXT: Git is required but was not found on PATH. Please install Git and try again.

[MESSAGE not-a-repo]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: The target path is not a Git repository. Choose a repository or initialize one first.

[MESSAGE tree-not-clean]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: The working tree has uncommitted changes. This operation was declined to protect them; commit or stash first.

[MESSAGE detached-head]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: HEAD is detached, so no branch can be inferred. Please name the branch explicitly.

[MESSAGE nothing-staged]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: There are no staged changes to commit. Stage the intended paths and try again.

[MESSAGE message-required]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: A commit message is required. Please provide one.

[MESSAGE pathspec-required]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: At least one pathspec is required for this operation.

[MESSAGE bad-argument]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: An argument was not understood. Please review the usage and try again.

[MESSAGE unknown-command]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: That command is not recognized. See the usage for the available commands.

# --- Resource / integrity concerns -------------------------------------------
[MESSAGE size-ceiling]
CONCERN: size-ceiling
TEXT: A single item exceeds the 200 MiB transaction ceiling and cannot be split, so the plan cannot proceed. Please reduce the item or adjust the plan.

[MESSAGE memory-bloat]
CONCERN: memory-bloat
SEVERITY: warning
TEXT: Memory use has grown beyond the advisory budget for this operation. Consider working in smaller batches.

[MESSAGE disk-space]
CONCERN: disk-space
TEXT: There is not enough free disk space to complete this operation safely. Please free space and try again.

[MESSAGE missing-file]
CONCERN: missing-file
TEXT: An expected file or object could not be found. Please confirm the path and repository state.

[MESSAGE corruption]
CONCERN: corruption
SEVERITY: fatal
TEXT: An integrity check failed, which suggests a damaged file or object. No changes were made; please run a repository check before continuing.

[MESSAGE overflow]
CONCERN: arithmetic-overflow
TEXT: A size or count calculation would overflow and was rejected rather than allowed to wrap. Please reduce the scope of the request.

[MESSAGE permission]
CONCERN: permission
TEXT: Permission was denied for this action. Please check file and remote permissions and try again.

[MESSAGE no-digest-tool]
CONCERN: precondition
TEXT: No suitable checksum tool (SHA-256 or SHA-512) is available, so an integrity reference cannot be computed.

# --- Transport / resume progress ---------------------------------------------
[MESSAGE resume-interrupted]
CONCERN: network-loss
SEVERITY: note
TEXT: The connection was interrupted; the remaining work will resume from the last acknowledged point.

[MESSAGE resume-halted]
CONCERN: network-loss
SEVERITY: error
TEXT: The retry limit was reached with work still remaining, so the effort was halted rather than looping. Please retry when the connection is stable.

[MESSAGE resume-complete]
CONCERN: none
SEVERITY: info
STREAM: stdout
TEXT: The remote has acknowledged all work; the transfer is complete.

# =============================================================================
# MAP
# =============================================================================
# The tree-wide print listener (git/gitmsg-listen.h, git/gitmsg-listen.c)
# intercepts every stdout/stderr print in the Edition Git. For each intercepted
# write it knows the call site (source file, function) and the rendered text,
# and consults the rules below to decide whether that write should be replaced
# by a catalogued message. This is how "when we get an output to print, we take
# the call site, then decide the real message" is expressed as config.
#
# Each rule is one `MAP:` line with whitespace-separated `key=value` selectors
# and a `->` target message id:
#
#   MAP: [file=<substr>] [func=<substr>] [text=<substr>] -> <message-id>
#
# Selector semantics:
#   file - matches when the call-site source file path contains <substr>.
#   func - matches when the call-site function name contains <substr>.
#   text - matches when the rendered output contains <substr>.
# An omitted selector matches anything; an empty selector matches anything.
# Rules are evaluated top to bottom; the FIRST rule that matches all of its
# present selectors wins. The target must be a message id defined above.
#
# DEFAULT BEHAVIOUR: with no MAP rules (the shipped default), every intercepted
# write passes through verbatim, so enabling the listener changes no output.
# Git's data output (git log / diff / cat-file / the pack protocol) is only
# ever altered where a rule explicitly maps it. A rule that resolves to an
# error/fatal message is never allowed to route it to stdout; such a match is
# refused and the original bytes pass through, so a diagnostic is never hidden.
#
# Examples (commented out; uncomment and adapt to opt in):
#
# # A no-space-left write from the object layer -> the mature disk-space message.
# MAP: text=No space left on device -> disk-space
#
# # A "unable to read" diagnostic from the loose-object reader -> missing-file.
# MAP: file=object-file.c text=unable to read -> missing-file
#
# # A specific helper's permission complaint -> the permission message.
# MAP: func=lock_file text=Permission denied -> permission

# RULE: Advisory presentation only. This config re-words, re-streams, or
# localizes output; it never changes an operation's behaviour, never turns an
# error into a success, never suppresses a safety refusal, never hides a
# diagnostic by routing it to stdout, and never authorizes a destructive
# action. A stale, missing, or altered config falls back to the compiled
# defaults in git/messages.h.
